rmusic","list_year":null,"rank":26,"year":null,"note":null,"total":200,"scaruffi":null},{"id":3080531282495,"name":"AOTY Must Hear 1970s","src":"musthear","grp":null,"list_year":null,"rank":74,"year":null,"note":null,"total":256,"scaruffi":null},{"id":192934750972716,"name":"1001 Albums You Must Hear Before You Die","src":"charts","grp":"1001 Albums","list_year":null,"rank":365,"year":1976,"note":null,"total":999,"scaruffi":null},{"id":65232636722211,"name":"Scaruffi 1970s","src":"charts","grp":"Scaruffi","list_year":null,"rank":508,"year":1976,"note":null,"total":572,"scaruffi":null}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Released in 1976, this album is the third in Stevie Wonder's string of three career-defining works. Motown Founder Barry Gordy lauded the album, stating that Stevie channeled all his life experiences into it, and \"it worked.\" The album includes the quintessential jazz rock fusion song \"Contusion\" and the R&B tribute \"Sir Duke.\" \"In 1976, Stevie released the album Songs and the Key of Life. This is the third in his string of three career defining albums, beginning with Intervisions in 1972, Fulfillingness's First Finale in 1974, and now the Songs of Key of Life.\"","year":1976,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 38 - The Influence of Jazz on 70s Popular Music","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-38-the-influence-of-jazz-on-70s-popular-music"},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 38 - The Influence of Jazz on 70s Popular Music","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"This \"double LP Magnum Opus\" was delivered 18 months after \"Fulfillingness' First Finale\" and was a landmark release, opening on the charts at number one, a first for Stevie Wonder. It went on to win the Grammy for Album of the Year in 1977, completing his unprecedented three-peat of Album of the Year awards. \"18 months later, when Wonder belatedly delivered his double LP Magnum Opus songs in the key of life, the album opened on the charts at number one, a first for Stevie Wonder. And of course, took the Grammy the following way to.\"","year":1976,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"And the Grammy Goes to… Edition Part 1","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4ef0154465cd600c4e3a"},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"And the Grammy Goes to… Edition Part 1","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"This double LP, released in 1976, contained 26 songs, though Wonder recorded hundreds during the two-year period following his previous album. Motown founder Berry Gordy gave Wonder complete freedom and significant funds to create the album, allowing him to infuse his life experiences into the songs. Rolling Stone ranks it as the fourth greatest album of all time. Despite an initially mixed review from disco guru Vince Aletti, it won the Album of the Year Grammy Award. \"26 songs on a double LP although he recorded hundreds of songs during that two year period he worked all day every day.\"","year":1976,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 31 - The Spirit of '76 - Pop Music on America's Bicentennial","url":null}]}],"book_tracks":[{"id":176738525421912,"artist":"Stevie Wonder","artist_id":40848531602698,"title":"Songs in the Key of Life","year":1976,"ess":1,"yt":"CClqS7EJogQ","kind":null,"book":{"id":4,"title":"Funk: The Music, the People, and the Rhythm of the One","author":"Rickey Vincent","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/Funk/"},"scene":"El soul se vuelve funky","scene_id":154312107829352,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/Funk/#soul-funky","chapters":["Cap. 10: Soul funky: Express Yourself","Discografía: Discografía esencial"],"pages":[[1,145]]},{"id":176738525421912,"artist":"Stevie Wonder","artist_id":40848531602698,"title":"Songs In The Key Of Life","year":1976,"ess":1,"yt":"HrJB-MItdVY","kind":"a","book":{"i